It's more convenient that way, and safer once you get far enough to
meet monsters that can curse your inventory.  It doesn't really make
all _that_ much difference, but I like to bless mine anyway.

>> Other important things to bless are magic lamps and smoky potions,
>
> Only if you want to get a wish out of these. Used as an infinite source
> of light a magic lamp may as well stay even cursed.

I do like to keep a magic lamp as a light source, but I still bless
it, just in case I should ever decide I want the wish after all.

>> and the really hard ones aren't genocidable anyway.
>
> Well, not exactly. There are a lot hard monsters that you can genocide
> and can make your life easier and your progression faster; minotauri,
> all grades of vampires, the tougher quadrupeds, all grades of liches,
> Olog-Hai, jabberwock, ...

Minotaurs I might consider, though they don't bother my current Valk
at all. :-)  There are ways for even physically weaker characters to
deal with them, though.  (Hint: MR 0)

Vampires are utterly harmless if you have Excalibur or some other
source of level drain resistance.  They're also easily kept away.

All quadrupeds, except leocrottas, are slow or normal speed.  By the
time you meet the tougher ones, you should be fast or very fast, so
you can run away from them and even, with some practice, keep hitting
them without giving them a chance to hit back.  They also respect the
E-word.  They do need to be dealt with very carefully when they first
appear, but after a while they become merely mobile food sources.

Liches I do genocide if I can, their spellcasting is too much of a
nuisance.  Can't say much about Olog-hai, to me they're just trolls
with better AC -- annoying like all T, but not worth genociding.
Jabberwocks make good pets.

Of course, these are all just reasons why _I_ don't normally genocide
those monsters.  Your mileage may, and probably will, vary.

